\section*{Abstract}

Telix is a design proposal for a general-purpose operating system
that combines four lines of research none of which has previously
appeared in a single system: ABI-compatible page clustering and
superpaging for a sublinear-metadata, high-TLB-reach memory
subsystem; a four-layer isolation hierarchy (cluster node →
multikernel partition → framekernel domain → userspace server)
that aligns protection boundaries with hardware-affinity boundaries
at each level; M:N threading revived on stackless Rust futures
with io\_uring-style completion rings; and a decomposed
formal-verification strategy in which the hardware model
(Tessera) is proved independently of the kernel and bridged by
Iris separation-logic resources.

The central thesis is that page clustering and superpaging solve
each other's weaknesses — clustering eliminates the external
fragmentation that makes superpage allocation unreliable, while
superpaging converts clustered extents into TLB reach — and that
no existing operating system has been designed around this
interaction.  The memory subsystem is built on extent-based
(morsel) accounting with no global page-descriptor array, supports
software-managed inverted page tables for architectures that
benefit from them, and includes a research-grade RISC-V MMU
extension with 42 distinct translation sizes.

The verification target is the full set of algorithms that interact
with the MMU, IOMMU, and interrupt controller: page-table
manipulation, TLB shootdown, IPI delivery, and LLFree allocation.
Hardware invariants are proved in Rocq/Iris (\textsc{Tessera});
kernel functions are specified manually in Iris heap\_lang and
proved against those invariants, function by function.

The system is designed for ABI compatibility with Linux, Windows,
and other operating systems through userspace personality servers,
giving it a practical path to real workloads rather than purely
laboratory interest.  Target architectures include RISC-V, AArch64,
LoongArch, MIPS, and x86-64, with CHERI as an additional isolation
boundary where available.
